title: 'Governance-by-tense: present→Cid, past→Loid (the firewall against PM-bureaucracy)'
summary: 'Reusable method from settling task-structure governance (TD-2026-06-14-467): when asked ''do we need a new agent?'', map who already WRITES each operation in code, then name the unowned gaps. Every gap turned out to be an operation, not a role, so they distributed onto existing owners by TENSE — present-tense task fields (claimant, blocked_on, DAG shape) to Cid the captain; past-tense (settledAt, calibration, archival) to Loid the learning officer; they never co-write a field. That single heuristic, recorded in both their global notebooks, is the firewall that makes ''no new PM agent'' self-enforcing. Global-notebook scope is the right home for cross-project agent BEHAVIORS vs project lore for project facts.'
